(1977087, 268348, 30364, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by bear_music:</b></div><hr/><i> This shot, IMO, is vastly superior to the "planned" shot in every way. Were I voting on the one in your cooments, I''d give it 2 points lower. The cropping on this one is very tense &amp; strange, the pool itself is otherwordly in a way that would not be possible if you showed it in its entirety and in context. Congrats on your red! </i></td></tr></table> <br/>I agree.  This is superior because of the tension bear identified.  As a result, one cannot simply pass by; it demands some involvement from the viewer.  Great image, Keith!</td>', 'Originally posted by bear_music: This shot, IMO, is vastly superior to the "planned" shot in every way. Were I voting on the one in your cooments, I''d give it 2 points lower. The cropping on this one is very tense & strange, the pool itself is otherwordly in a way that would not be possible if you showed it in its entirety and in context. Congrats on your red!  I agree.  This is superior because of the tension bear identified.  As a result, one cannot simply pass by; it demands some involvement from the viewer.  Great image, Keith!', '2005-12-25 16:46:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(1977203, 266724, 34938, '<td valign="top">Should have won a ribbon for this. absolutely gorgeous shot<br/></td>', 'Should have won a ribbon for this. absolutely gorgeous shot', '2005-12-25 19:38:23.000000', NULL, 0),
(1978027, 269016, 37779, '<td valign="top">Man, you are some kind of nekked boy. Beautiful photo, thought it would score a lot higher. Keep up the amazing work, sir.</td>', 'Man, you are some kind of nekked boy. Beautiful photo, thought it would score a lot higher. Keep up the amazing work, sir.', '2005-12-26 01:49:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(1978417, 268883, 24940, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by chaimelle:</b></div><hr/><i> I guess the results will stand, but I am very disappointed to learn that the shot was not taken between 4 and 5 AM.  Kind of seems like cheating to me. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>This may alienate some fellow DPC-ers but so be it.<br/> <br/>All those who complain so loudly about the "time" aspect of your photograph overlook an immensely important fact:  Nowhere in the description of the challenge is a time zone specified.  So 4am or 5am in one place is some other time somewhere else.  It''s all relative and really doesn''t matter one bit.  You made a BEAUTIFUL image by creating a great illusion - pretty much what photography (other than the strictest scientific or forensic kind) is all about, wouldn''t you say??<br/> <br/>Buen trabajo, Patrinus!</td>', 'Originally posted by chaimelle: I guess the results will stand, but I am very disappointed to learn that the shot was not taken between 4 and 5 AM.  Kind of seems like cheating to me.   This may alienate some fellow DPC-ers but so be it. All those who complain so loudly about the "time" aspect of your photograph overlook an immensely important fact:  Nowhere in the description of the challenge is a time zone specified.  So 4am or 5am in one place is some other time somewhere else.  It''s all relative and really doesn''t matter one bit.  You made a BEAUTIFUL image by creating a great illusion - pretty much what photography (other than the strictest scientific or forensic kind) is all about, wouldn''t you say?? Buen trabajo, Patrinus!', '2005-12-26 08:15:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(1978907, 268883, 50127, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by saiphfire:</b></div><hr/><i> The description did not say "Take a shot that LOOKS as if it was taken between 4 and 5 am." </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>So if the opposite had happened.... someone awoke at 4 am and took a picture indoors that had no illusion of any time of day, but then told you that it was most definitely taken at 4 am and thus fits the challenege...then that would have been ok?<br/> <br/>All I have to say is... it''s 4am somewhere.<br/>Great photo!</td>', 'Originally posted by saiphfire: The description did not say "Take a shot that LOOKS as if it was taken between 4 and 5 am."   So if the opposite had happened.... someone awoke at 4 am and took a picture indoors that had no illusion of any time of day, but then told you that it was most definitely taken at 4 am and thus fits the challenege...then that would have been ok? All I have to say is... it''s 4am somewhere.Great photo!', '2005-12-26 13:40:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(1979132, 268883, 39221, '<td valign="top">Its a wonderfull shot, but if everything as below is true (wasn''t taken during challenge) then it needs to be disqualified.<br/> <br/>Regardless of whether you think it `looks` like it was taken at that time... it wasn''t - and thats the simple fact.<br/> <br/>Keep up the fantastic photo''s though :-)</td>', 'Its a wonderfull shot, but if everything as below is true (wasn''t taken during challenge) then it needs to be disqualified. Regardless of whether you think it `looks` like it was taken at that time... it wasn''t - and thats the simple fact. Keep up the fantastic photo''s though :-)', '2005-12-26 15:55:51.000000', NULL, 0),
(1979738, 267110, 16739, '<td valign="top">Are you going to offer this photo for sale ?</td>', 'Are you going to offer this photo for sale ?', '2005-12-26 20:46:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(1979891, 266868, 36076, '<td valign="top">eerie yet beautiful</td>', 'eerie yet beautiful', '2005-12-26 22:22:43.000000', NULL, 0),
(1979903, 267236, 37893, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/>The image in itself lacks the "punch" that most DPCers are looking for in all our challenges. The use of BW, in my opinion, added to the lack luster display that most people saw during the voting process.<br/> <br/>The image for one is too dark and the main focus, which is the monitor of your television, is too bright and lacks definition. there is an amount of visible noise that is apparent in this image. <br/> <br/>In my opinion, if this image were in color as opposed to a desaturated image, there would have been a bit more interest. The silhouette is great and adds visual drama however, the bright screen overpowers this and most DPCers including myself will just give this a 4 and move on.<br/> <br/>Light and detail is the key to make your audience stop and let the image sink in. As always, these are just my personal opinions on this image. Please take it with a grain of salt. We are all out here to learn ;)<br/> <br/>I have seen your portfolio and there is promise of definitely breaking out of the 4 slump. Keep on shooting and continue to learn from the folks in our group!<br/> <br/>Rikki</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  The image in itself lacks the "punch" that most DPCers are looking for in all our challenges. The use of BW, in my opinion, added to the lack luster display that most people saw during the voting process. The image for one is too dark and the main focus, which is the monitor of your television, is too bright and lacks definition. there is an amount of visible noise that is apparent in this image.  In my opinion, if this image were in color as opposed to a desaturated image, there would have been a bit more interest. The silhouette is great and adds visual drama however, the bright screen overpowers this and most DPCers including myself will just give this a 4 and move on. Light and detail is the key to make your audience stop and let the image sink in. As always, these are just my personal opinions on this image. Please take it with a grain of salt. We are all out here to learn ;) I have seen your portfolio and there is promise of definitely breaking out of the 4 slump. Keep on shooting and continue to learn from the folks in our group! Rikki', '2005-12-26 22:32:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(1979917, 268974, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Fabulous image.  I find that you never know what interpretation of the surroundings you''ll get with blacklight, when it lights up an area.  It is an exciting tool to use and you used it well with the white background. <br/> <br/>Composition is fine and the image tells a great story.  The use of grain of course adds drama and tension to the image of the terrified woman.<br/> <br/>It''s impossible to judge what our voters will see in an image.  Many were stopped by it and felt moved to comment.  To me, this was a high compliment to your art.<br/> <br/>The score?  Well, take it as a badge of honor.  <br/> <br/>You could win awards with this image.  And I hope you do.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Fabulous image.  I find that you never know what interpretation of the surroundings you''ll get with blacklight, when it lights up an area.  It is an exciting tool to use and you used it well with the white background.  Composition is fine and the image tells a great story.  The use of grain of course adds drama and tension to the image of the terrified woman. It''s impossible to judge what our voters will see in an image.  Many were stopped by it and felt moved to comment.  To me, this was a high compliment to your art. The score?  Well, take it as a badge of honor.   You could win awards with this image.  And I hope you do. ', '2005-12-26 22:39:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(1979961, 267245, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hello.  This is your first Challenge and you have done well.  You have a strong image here and one that, in my opinion, meets the Challenge well.  It is well photographed, for the most part, and the composition is beautiful.  And you have an understanding mother to allow you to photograph her at such an early hour of the day.  <br/> <br/>I think you know the area around the model''s nose is ''hot'' but you couldn''t do much about it with Basic Editing.  The remainder of the technicals, to me, are just fine.  <br/> <br/>For some reason - and it happens - our voters sometimes overlook a good image.  I think they did so with this one.<br/> <br/>I wish you much success in your participation in DPC.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Hello.  This is your first Challenge and you have done well.  You have a strong image here and one that, in my opinion, meets the Challenge well.  It is well photographed, for the most part, and the composition is beautiful.  And you have an understanding mother to allow you to photograph her at such an early hour of the day.   I think you know the area around the model''s nose is ''hot'' but you couldn''t do much about it with Basic Editing.  The remainder of the technicals, to me, are just fine.   For some reason - and it happens - our voters sometimes overlook a good image.  I think they did so with this one. I wish you much success in your participation in DPC.', '2005-12-26 22:54:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(1980016, 268883, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hello.  Congratulations on your ribbon.  You have received many comments on your image and many of your commentors can give you better technical advice than I can, so I will not attempt to do so here.  <br/> <br/>Oddly enough (and as I mentioned in a thread somewhere relating to this image) birds <b>do</b> fly at night, especially large birds during the migratory seasons.  The heat of the sun would dehydrate the birds quickly and they could not travel the long miles they do if they attempted to migrate during the daylight.<br/> <br/>Anyhow, this is a competent and compelling image and you were nicely rewarded for it.  <br/> <br/>I wish you continued success in your DPC Challenges.<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Hello.  Congratulations on your ribbon.  You have received many comments on your image and many of your commentors can give you better technical advice than I can, so I will not attempt to do so here.   Oddly enough (and as I mentioned in a thread somewhere relating to this image) birds do fly at night, especially large birds during the migratory seasons.  The heat of the sun would dehydrate the birds quickly and they could not travel the long miles they do if they attempted to migrate during the daylight. Anyhow, this is a competent and compelling image and you were nicely rewarded for it.   I wish you continued success in your DPC Challenges.', '2005-12-26 23:14:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(1980039, 267106, 16648, '<td valign="top">Critique Club checking in here.<br/> <br/>Several things jump out at me with this pic - no focal point and the two lights on the left - the flare thing and the lower left street light - jump out and draw me eye something fierce. The WB is off - all the lights are too yellow. The top 1/3 of the pic is just nothingness. General rule on composition is that unless it adds to the pic, remove it.<br/> <br/>I see several things that might have been made a focal point - two bridges, the leading line of the street might have been good being empty at 5 am. There is something interesting left middle above the roof of the foreground house that looks like it might be a train station or something - Bottom line - what am i supposed to be looking at? What is the message, the story, this image is to convey to me?<br/> <br/>I see the voters gave yo ua 5.9! Man, i gotta do more night (OK, SOME!) night shots as the voters are suckers for them. I found out in the landscape challenge (see my entry and alanfreed ''s entry to see what i mean). <br/>-chris</td>', 'Critique Club checking in here. Several things jump out at me with this pic - no focal point and the two lights on the left - the flare thing and the lower left street light - jump out and draw me eye something fierce. The WB is off - all the lights are too yellow. The top 1/3 of the pic is just nothingness. General rule on composition is that unless it adds to the pic, remove it. I see several things that might have been made a focal point - two bridges, the leading line of the street might have been good being empty at 5 am. There is something interesting left middle above the roof of the foreground house that looks like it might be a train station or something - Bottom line - what am i supposed to be looking at? What is the message, the story, this image is to convey to me? I see the voters gave yo ua 5.9! Man, i gotta do more night (OK, SOME!) night shots as the voters are suckers for them. I found out in the landscape challenge (see my entry and alanfreed ''s entry to see what i mean). -chris', '2005-12-26 23:24:39.000000', NULL, 0),
(1980067, 268936, 18450, '<td valign="top">Hi Matt, Greetings from the critique club. <br/> <br/>Not a lot I can say about this superb shot that already hasn''t been said. It grabs your attention immediately with it''s vibrant colours, composition is excellent. Certainly meets the challenge well. <br/> <br/>The only thing that could have improved this for me is to have seen more of the boats, the reflection of the sun and sky in the water. <br/> <br/>A very beautiful, well done sunrise image that would look great decorating a wall. Congrats on your well deserved 7th place. Good work!!<br/> <br/>sue<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-12-26 23:34:34.</i></td>', 'Hi Matt, Greetings from the critique club.  Not a lot I can say about this superb shot that already hasn''t been said. It grabs your attention immediately with it''s vibrant colours, composition is excellent. Certainly meets the challenge well.  The only thing that could have improved this for me is to have seen more of the boats, the reflection of the sun and sky in the water.  A very beautiful, well done sunrise image that would look great decorating a wall. Congrats on your well deserved 7th place. Good work!! sue', '2005-12-26 23:32:18.000000', '2005-12-26 23:34:34.000000', 0),
(1980913, 268751, 37893, '<td valign="top"><b>Greeting from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>First of all I must say, like everyone else perhaps, your model looks strikingly great for someone who has been up since 4am ;)<br/> <br/>On to the show. The overall tonalities of this image struck me best at first. As my eye pans around the image, mostly on her face of course, the contrast and tonalities are spot on. I really do like the approach of using BW for this image. The smoothness in her skin is evident and the texture in her hair is quite rich and pleasing to the eye.<br/> <br/>My only drawback is the background. IMHO, had it been all white, this would have probably been a higher rated image. I would probably add anywhere between a .1 to a .3 to this image. It is almost a high key image and I''m quite fond of it myself and so are a few folks here on DPC. There is a hot spot on her left hand but nothing really to downgarde this beautiful image.<br/> <br/>Overall I think your score reflects your protrayal of this image for this challenge. I love the image and rated it fairly high (8). Keep on shooting and I can''t wait to see more of your work. <br/> <br/>Rikki</td>', 'Greeting from the Critique Club  First of all I must say, like everyone else perhaps, your model looks strikingly great for someone who has been up since 4am ;) On to the show. The overall tonalities of this image struck me best at first. As my eye pans around the image, mostly on her face of course, the contrast and tonalities are spot on. I really do like the approach of using BW for this image. The smoothness in her skin is evident and the texture in her hair is quite rich and pleasing to the eye. My only drawback is the background. IMHO, had it been all white, this would have probably been a higher rated image. I would probably add anywhere between a .1 to a .3 to this image. It is almost a high key image and I''m quite fond of it myself and so are a few folks here on DPC. There is a hot spot on her left hand but nothing really to downgarde this beautiful image. Overall I think your score reflects your protrayal of this image for this challenge. I love the image and rated it fairly high (8). Keep on shooting and I can''t wait to see more of your work.  Rikki', '2005-12-27 10:54:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(1981731, 267033, 6900, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hi Joshua - you got some pretty good comments about the blown out spots and the lunar feel, so I won''t spend too much of your time telling you what I like - the focus feels slightly off and despite the insanely bright lights, the flag isn''t quite bright enough for my tastes.  I think I''d have tried a vertical here - between the lights. You would get more light on the flag, have even more detail in the ground and still keep the lunar feel to it.  I like this shot a lot - just not sure about those few things.  Also - how much did you Neat Image?  Part of the blur may be that.<br/> <br/>Happy shooting!<br/>Matt</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Hi Joshua - you got some pretty good comments about the blown out spots and the lunar feel, so I won''t spend too much of your time telling you what I like - the focus feels slightly off and despite the insanely bright lights, the flag isn''t quite bright enough for my tastes.  I think I''d have tried a vertical here - between the lights. You would get more light on the flag, have even more detail in the ground and still keep the lunar feel to it.  I like this shot a lot - just not sure about those few things.  Also - how much did you Neat Image?  Part of the blur may be that. Happy shooting!Matt', '2005-12-27 16:49:38.000000', NULL, 0),
(1981742, 267997, 47912, '<td valign="top">Fab photo Matt, congratulations</td>', 'Fab photo Matt, congratulations', '2005-12-27 16:56:50.000000', NULL, 0),
(1981770, 269016, 3936, '<td valign="top">Critique Club Review:<br/>Nice tight composition.  Taking the background to white works well.  However, there are a couple of spots on the model (just below the left hand and midthigh on the right leg, where the detail has burned out.  Great depth of field, and except for the two small areas mentioned, very good tonal control.  Nudes are always risky in a challenge.  Should have scored higher.</td>', 'Critique Club Review:Nice tight composition.  Taking the background to white works well.  However, there are a couple of spots on the model (just below the left hand and midthigh on the right leg, where the detail has burned out.  Great depth of field, and except for the two small areas mentioned, very good tonal control.  Nudes are always risky in a challenge.  Should have scored higher.', '2005-12-27 17:23:53.000000', NULL, 0),
(1981855, 268338, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>This image is peaceful and quiet.  I like the composition very much and the light on the pond is beautiful.  Because it is backlit, it is difficult to make out the railway bridge and while I think I see that there is actually a train on it, as a title, it is just a little obscure.<br/> <br/>I would suggest a couple of ways to retake this image.  First, if you do try again, consider bracketing your shots.  In this instance, definitely under-expose to see if that would give you a better image.  I would also experiment with your ISO.  It may be that an ISO of 100 would do the job well here.<br/> <br/>The final suggestion I would make would be to try to use the full amount of pixels DPC allows.  This image comes across as a bit small but in DPC you have 640 pixels on the longest side as well as the 150mb file size.  You might try to take full advantage of those pixels.<br/> <br/>Congratulations for getting up so early to take this pretty shot.  I wish you luck in future Challenges.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  This image is peaceful and quiet.  I like the composition very much and the light on the pond is beautiful.  Because it is backlit, it is difficult to make out the railway bridge and while I think I see that there is actually a train on it, as a title, it is just a little obscure. I would suggest a couple of ways to retake this image.  First, if you do try again, consider bracketing your shots.  In this instance, definitely under-expose to see if that would give you a better image.  I would also experiment with your ISO.  It may be that an ISO of 100 would do the job well here. The final suggestion I would make would be to try to use the full amount of pixels DPC allows.  This image comes across as a bit small but in DPC you have 640 pixels on the longest side as well as the 150mb file size.  You might try to take full advantage of those pixels. Congratulations for getting up so early to take this pretty shot.  I wish you luck in future Challenges.', '2005-12-27 18:20:46.000000', NULL, 0),
(1981932, 266940, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Well, Tofarley, it looks as if this is your week to be Critiqued by SFAlice.  I see I just did your nice Candlelight Romance entry.<br/> <br/>This one is a different mood entirely.  It''s humerous and technically good.  (The more I see of the S2 IS at work,the more I like it.)  Composition is good.  <br/> <br/>The voters generally liked it and you received a pretty good score.  I guess your very nice image just didn''t have that extra something that voters tend to reward so highly.<br/> <br/>I wish you continued enjoyment in your DPC experience.  <br/> <br/>And I do hope you find our comments helpful.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Well, Tofarley, it looks as if this is your week to be Critiqued by SFAlice.  I see I just did your nice Candlelight Romance entry. This one is a different mood entirely.  It''s humerous and technically good.  (The more I see of the S2 IS at work,the more I like it.)  Composition is good.   The voters generally liked it and you received a pretty good score.  I guess your very nice image just didn''t have that extra something that voters tend to reward so highly. I wish you continued enjoyment in your DPC experience.   And I do hope you find our comments helpful.', '2005-12-27 18:50:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(1982042, 269004, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Hello, Tony.  This is your week to be critiqued by SFAlice, I guess.  You hve a lovely image here and you were well rewarded by our voters.  It''s an equisite sunrise with glorious colors.  You made the most of them.  Your horizon''s straight (I  checked) and your composition is lovely.  Technicals, so far as I can tell, are fine.<br/> <br/>You''ve made this Critique easy.  It''s a good image and you should be very, very happy with it.  Perhaps it didn''t have the extra sparkle that earns you a spot on the front page, but it, in it''s own way, is lovely.<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Hello, Tony.  This is your week to be critiqued by SFAlice, I guess.  You hve a lovely image here and you were well rewarded by our voters.  It''s an equisite sunrise with glorious colors.  You made the most of them.  Your horizon''s straight (I  checked) and your composition is lovely.  Technicals, so far as I can tell, are fine. You''ve made this Critique easy.  It''s a good image and you should be very, very happy with it.  Perhaps it didn''t have the extra sparkle that earns you a spot on the front page, but it, in it''s own way, is lovely. Keep up the good work.', '2005-12-27 19:34:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(1982084, 268709, 14476, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club</b> <br/> <br/>Wow.  I am so pleased to have the opportunity to look at this fascinating image one more time.  Of course it is beautiful and you were well rewarded by our voters for your efforts.<br/> <br/>There''s not much I can add to those comments you have already received, so I''ll just say "thanks, again" and move on to the next person in line for a Critique.<br/> <br/>Still, (as an avid SciFi reader) it was a pleasure to examine this image so -er - closely.  I can almost find the Heinlein homesteads on the topo you have provided.<br/> <br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club  Wow.  I am so pleased to have the opportunity to look at this fascinating image one more time.  Of course it is beautiful and you were well rewarded by our voters for your efforts. There''s not much I can add to those comments you have already received, so I''ll just say "thanks, again" and move on to the next person in line for a Critique. Still, (as an avid SciFi reader) it was a pleasure to examine this image so -er - closely.  I can almost find the Heinlein homesteads on the topo you have provided. ', '2005-12-27 19:46:51.000000', NULL, 0),
